2026-09-28T10:55:30-07:00 CODE    tools/decode_uart.py added: parses MEAS lines from a sim transcript or a raw /dev/tang-uart capture (identical bytes), computes per-phase rate = cmd_count x B/cmd / pclk x f_pclk, emits JSON. Refuses (rc=1) on missing snapshots, non-monotonic counters, or zero commands; rc=2 when no MEAS lines exist. pclk defaults to 99.5625 MHz, the real hardware value, not ddr3_top's rounded FREQ=99_800_000.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 SIM     Replaced vendor prim_sim.v with first-party models (simulation/gowin_prim_models.v). prim_sim.v under Verilator --timing advanced 61 us in 180 s; the first-party models run 100 ms in ~16 s.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 SIM     Five real bugs found and fixed in the models, each recorded in the file: (1) OSER8 shift register reloaded from a pclk-latched copy, so every word arrived one pclk late and MR0/MR1 fell out of init; (2) command quadrant advanced per fclk EDGE instead of per CYCLE, so every command decoded as NOP; (3) rPLL built from dual-edge divider chains produced pclk at 27 kHz, not 99.5625 MHz; (4) the two rPLL clocks shared one always block, making the #delays sequential and both clocks the same wrong frequency; (5) testbench used #500000000 with timescale 1ps -- 500 us, not 500 ms -- ending the run 1000x early.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 SIM     Added simulation/probe_clocks.v, which ASSERTS pclk=99.5625 MHz, fclk=398.25 MHz and fclk/pclk=4.0 to 0.1%. It caught bug (4). A dual-edge chain that is wrong by 3687x produces no warning anywhere else.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 RESULT  MEASURED, WRITE, FROM THE DUT'S OWN COUNTERS. WIPE 8.0838 pclk/cmd, WRITE_BLOCK 8.0835 pclk/cmd, 65536 accepted commands each, 24.63 MB/s at 2 B/cmd. The two phases agree to 4 decimal places.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 RESULT  This SUPERSEDES the design note's predicted 7.0587 pclk/cmd / 28.45 MB/s, which was an analysis. Measured is 14.5% slower. Step 2 existed to replace that inference with a measurement, and it did.
2026-09-28T11:12:40-07:00 BLOCKED READ NOT MEASURED. VERIFY_BLOCK aborts on the first dout mismatch, so the s3 snapshot is never taken and MEAS3 is all zeros. tools/decode_uart.py correctly refuses the run (rc=1) rather than reporting a partial. Cause is the functional pass-through DQ path in the first-party IDES8_MEM/DQS models, not the controller: the write rate is a pure FSM property and does not touch the data path, which is why it is trustworthy and the read rate is not.
